1	// The FRESHNESS half of the fleet-roster sweep: the root-cause classification of a
2	// member's mount against canon, and the freshness section of the run report.
3	//
4	// It holds no enumeration and no membership classification — the roster is built once,
5	// for both halves, by its sibling check-fleet-roster.mjs, which hands this module only
6	// the members it has already established are covered, in scope and this sweep's to
7	// measure. What lives here is everything specific to the freshness QUESTION. Its
8	// counterpart is adoption-issues.mjs, which owns the coverage question; the two never
9	// import each other.
10	//
11	// WHY THE QUESTION EXISTS. Under per-project scheduling every member maintains ITSELF:
12	// its own vendored `claudinite-scheduler.yml` fires hourly, and its `baselining` task
13	// re-vendors the mount from canon. That is the right architecture — and it removed the
14	// last thing that ever looked at a member from the outside. A member whose scheduler
15	// was never vendored, whose workflow was deleted, or whose baselining has been failing
16	// for a fortnight is otherwise invisible: it still carries a declaration, so the
17	// coverage half calls it covered, and it files no failure issue because nothing runs
18	// there to fail. Self-maintenance cannot detect its own absence.
19	//
20	// WHERE THE ANSWER GOES: the run report, and nowhere else. This half filed a
21	// `fleet-drift` issue per unhealthy member until #1854. The dashboard's Drift tile
22	// measures the same fact from the same source — each member's declaration against
23	// canon — and recomputes on load, so the issues were a second surface for one
24	// question and the staler of the two: only ever as current as the last daily sweep,
25	// which is how a member that fell behind hours after a sweep sat unreported while
26	// three issues named members that had already caught up. The coverage half still
27	// files, because no other surface answers coverage.
28	//
29	// WHAT `behind` MEASURES: the VERSION GAP, and nothing else. The versioned update
30	// flows stamp `engineVersion` and `packVersions` and deliberately never rewrite `ref`
31	// or `updated`, so on a well-maintained member the stamped ref is frozen at whatever
32	// commit first vendored it and its AGE measures nothing at all. A date measure over
33	// that stamp does not decay gracefully either: every member's ref ages at the same
34	// rate, so one arbitrary day the whole fleet crosses the window at once and the sweep
35	// calls every repo behind for a fleet that is, by versions, current (#1025).
36	//
37	// So a member is behind when its stamped engine version is below canon's, or any pack
38	// it stamps is below that pack's manifest version in canon — read out of CANON over
39	// the API, never out of the enforcer's own mount, which is itself a member and can be
40	// behind. The stamped ref is still read for what it honestly is, provenance: whether
41	// it is a commit on canon's trunk at all, which is the #328 wedge.
42	//
43	// Read-only toward every repo it touches, the enforcer included: this half now writes
44	// nothing at all.
45	import { DECLARATION } from '../../fleet-api.mjs';
46	import { VERSION_SOURCE, versionFromLiteral, isVersion, versionAbove } from '../../../../engine/version.mjs';
47	import { installedVersions } from '../../../../engine/installed-versions.mjs';
48	
49	const SCHEDULER = '.github/workflows/claudinite-scheduler.yml';
50	
51	export { SCHEDULER };
52	export const FRESH = 'fresh';
53	
54	
55	// --- classification (pure) ----------------------------------------------------
56	
57	// `installed` is what the member's own settings say it holds ({ engineVersion,
58	// packVersions }) and `canon` the same two numbers read out of canon for exactly the
59	// packs that member names. Kept free of I/O so every branch is testable directly.
60	//
61	// THE REF IS GONE (#1252), and with it `ref-not-on-trunk`. That state asked whether
62	// the member's stamped ref was an ancestor of canon's default branch, because the
63	// anti-rewind guard used to refuse a converge over a ref it could not place — the
64	// guard now compares versions and needs no ref, so the wedge it reported cannot
65	// happen and the per-member compare call that detected it is a read nobody needs.
66	//
67	// The precedence is about ROOT CAUSE, not the order the facts arrive in: a member with
68	// no scheduler is ALSO behind, and reporting "behind" would send the reader chasing a
69	// symptom of the missing cron.
70	export function classifyFreshness({ hasScheduler, installed, canon, dormant = false }) {
71	  // Neither number means the mount has never been written by an engine that stamps —
72	  // which is every engine there has been since the versioned flows landed. A repo
73	  // whose declaration names packs whose versions it cannot state has not been
74	  // vendored, whatever else is true of it.
75	  const packIds = Object.keys(installed.packVersions);
76	  if (installed.engineVersion === null && packIds.length === 0) {
77	    return { state: 'no-stamp', detail: `${DECLARATION} carries no engineVersion and no pack versions — the repo declares packs but has never been vendored` };
78	  }
79	  // A DORMANT member's scheduler is stopped by its own declaration, so its absence is
80	  // obedience rather than drift, and this is the ONE state dormancy suppresses. The
81	  // version comparison below still runs: what the declaration bought was quiet about
82	  // the scheduler, not exemption from being measured.
83	  if (!hasScheduler && !dormant) {
84	    return { state: 'no-scheduler', detail: `no ${SCHEDULER} — the repo has no cron, so nothing there will ever converge it` };
85	  }
86	  // An absent canon number is not a zero: a pack retired from canon has no manifest
87	  // to be behind, and comparing against a missing entry would report every member
88	  // that still stamps it.
89	  const gaps = [];
90	  if (installed.engineVersion !== null && canon.engineVersion !== null && versionAbove(canon.engineVersion, installed.engineVersion)) {
91	    gaps.push(`engine v${installed.engineVersion} → v${canon.engineVersion}`);
92	  }
93	  for (const id of packIds.sort()) {
94	    const here = installed.packVersions[id];
95	    const there = canon.packVersions[id];
96	    if (isVersion(here) && isVersion(there) && versionAbove(there, here)) {
97	      gaps.push(`${id} v${here} → v${there}`);
98	    }
99	  }
100	  if (gaps.length) return { state: 'behind', detail: `behind canon by ${gaps.join(', ')}` };
101	  return {
102	    state: FRESH,
103	    detail: `engine v${installed.engineVersion ?? '—'}, ${packIds.length} declared pack(s) at canon versions`,
104	  };
105	}
106	
107	// --- canon's own version numbers ----------------------------------------------
108	
109	// What a member is measured AGAINST, read out of canon over the API. Not out of this
110	// repo's checkout: the enforcer runs a vendored mount like any other member, so its
111	// own engine/version.mjs says what the ENFORCER received, which is a different
112	// question and can be older.
113	//
114	// Both numbers are extracted from source with an anchored line match rather than by
115	// importing the modules, because the sweep has no canon checkout to import from and
116	// the two literals are the whole payload. A file whose number cannot be read THROWS —
117	// the caller turns that into UNKNOWN for the member, which fails the run, and a
118	// guessed version would silently reclassify the fleet.
119	//
120	// Memoized per reader, promise and all: one reader is built per sweep and every
121	// member consults it, so canon is read once per distinct pack rather than once per
122	// member per pack.
123	const ENGINE_VERSION_RE = new RegExp(String.raw`^export const ENGINE_VERSION = '?(${VERSION_SOURCE})'?;$`, 'm');
124	const PACK_VERSION_RE = new RegExp(String.raw`^ {2}version: '?(${VERSION_SOURCE})'?,$`, 'm');
125	
126	export function canonVersions(gh, canonRepo) {
127	  const packs = new Map();
128	  let engine = null;
129	  const source = async (path) => {
130	    const res = await gh(`/repos/${canonRepo}/contents/${encodeURI(path)}`);
131	    if (res.status === 404) return null;
132	    if (res.status !== 200 || typeof res.json?.content !== 'string') throw new Error(`${canonRepo}:${path} returned ${res.status}`);
133	    return Buffer.from(res.json.content, 'base64').toString('utf8');
134	  };
135	  return {
136	    engine() {
137	      engine ??= (async () => {
138	        const text = await source('engine/version.mjs');
139	        const m = text && ENGINE_VERSION_RE.exec(text);
140	        if (!m) throw new Error(`canon ${canonRepo} has no readable ENGINE_VERSION in engine/version.mjs`);
141	        return versionFromLiteral(m[1]);
142	      })();
143	      return engine;
144	    },
145	    // null when canon carries no such pack — a pack the member still stamps but canon
146	    // has retired. Distinct from a manifest that is there and unreadable, which throws.
147	    pack(id) {
148	      if (!packs.has(id)) {
149	        packs.set(id, (async () => {
150	          const text = await source(`packs/${id}/pack.mjs`);
151	          if (text === null) return null;
152	          const m = PACK_VERSION_RE.exec(text);
153	          if (!m) throw new Error(`canon ${canonRepo} has no readable version in packs/${id}/pack.mjs`);
154	          return versionFromLiteral(m[1]);
155	        })());
156	      }
157	      return packs.get(id);
158	    },
159	  };
160	}
161	
162	// --- the per-member mount probe -----------------------------------------------
163	
164	// The reads this half adds on top of the declaration the roster walk already made:
165	// the scheduler workflow's presence, canon's view of the stamped ref, and canon's
166	// version numbers for the packs this member stamps. It is handed the declaration
167	// rather than re-reading it, which is the whole point of the merge — the coverage half
168	// needed the same file, and a member was being read twice.
169	//
170	// `canon` is the shared reader built once per sweep, so the per-member cost of the
171	// version half is bounded by how many packs this member declares that no earlier
172	// member did — usually none.
173	//
174	// Throws on anything indeterminate; the caller turns that into UNKNOWN for this half
175	// alone. A member whose mount cannot be probed is still one whose declaration was read,
176	// so the coverage half keeps its verdict.
177	export async function probeMount(gh, fullName, declaration, { canon }) {
178	  const wf = await gh(`/repos/${fullName}/contents/${SCHEDULER}`);
179	  if (wf.status !== 200 && wf.status !== 404) throw new Error(`${SCHEDULER} check returned ${wf.status}`);
180	  const hasScheduler = wf.status === 200;
181	
182	  // Wherever this member spells them: the current shape, with the retired block read
183	  // underneath for one that has not run the #1252 record yet.
184	  const installed = installedVersions(declaration);
185	  const canonAt = { engineVersion: null, packVersions: {} };
186	  if (installed.engineVersion !== null || Object.keys(installed.packVersions).length > 0) {
187	    canonAt.engineVersion = await canon.engine();
188	    for (const id of Object.keys(installed.packVersions)) {
189	      const there = await canon.pack(id);
190	      if (there !== null) canonAt.packVersions[id] = there;
191	    }
192	  }
193	  return { hasScheduler, installed, canon: canonAt };
194	}
195	
196	// --- the freshness section of the report (pure) -------------------------------
197	
198	// Enumerates the FULL fleet: every repo lands in exactly one list — fresh (with how
199	// fresh), unhealthy (with its root cause), dormant, out of scope (with why), unknown —
200	// plus the two repos this half never measures, named rather than silently absent. A
201	// report that names only the failures leaves the reader unable to tell "fresh" from
202	// "fell out of the report". Kept free of I/O so the full-roster property is testable
203	// directly. `fresh` is `[{ fullName, detail }]`; `outOfScope` entries carry their
204	// reason inline.
205	export function renderFreshnessSummary({
206	  owner, home, canonRepo, canonBranch, fresh, unhealthy, dormant, outOfScope, unknown,
207	}) {
208	  const notMeasured = [`\`${home}\` — the enforcer, swept by its own scheduler`];
209	  if (canonRepo.toLowerCase() !== home.toLowerCase()) notMeasured.push(`\`${canonRepo}\` — canon, with no vendored mount to be stale`);
210	  return [
211	    `# Fleet freshness sweep — ${owner} (measured by stamped versions against canon: ${canonRepo}@${canonBranch})`,
212	    '',
213	    '| fresh | behind | no scheduler | no stamp | dormant | out of scope | unknown |',
214	    '| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |',
215	    `| ${fresh.length} | ${unhealthy.filter((u) => u.state === 'behind').length} | `
216	      + `${unhealthy.filter((u) => u.state === 'no-scheduler').length} | `
217	      + `${unhealthy.filter((u) => u.state === 'no-stamp').length} | `
218	      + `${dormant.length} | ${outOfScope.length} | ${unknown.length} |`,
219	    '',
220	    unhealthy.length
221	      ? `**Behind:**\n${unhealthy.map((u) => `- \`${u.fullName}\` — **${u.state}**: ${u.detail}`
222	        + `${u.dormant ? ' — dormant, so nothing there will converge this on its own' : ''}`).join('\n')}`
223	      : '**Every covered member is up to date 🎉**',
224	    fresh.length
